        "question": {
          ":question/id": "15bb7b0f-caf8-5208-b9f7-85629d611cef",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "![](/tmp/ca9837-templates-703c6brn/17592186096814/5898a494e070e5d102680f3db007a71177db0d25073aa35fccff7cc3a2a00300-prompt.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $m+n$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First, we have to find $m+n$. According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $m+n$ goes from the tail of $m$ to the head of $n$:\n\n![](/tmp/ca9837-templates-703c6brn/17592186096814/5898a494e070e5d102680f3db007a71177db0d25073aa35fccff7cc3a2a00300-solution.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=1.5$ and $BH=1.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $m+n$ is $\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|m+n|}\\cdot (m+n) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}(m+n)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          ":question/answer-fields": [
            {
              ":answer-field/id": "e71aa093-9f7a-5654-9331-55b22e46d4c8",
